  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nAn omega-like blocking pattern remains in place this morning, with \nthe axis of the upper level ridge centered directly over the UP \nbetween deep troughing centered over western Canada and the Mid \nAtlantic. Water vapor imagery reveals a weak disturbance riding up \nthe western periphery of the ridge, bringing extensive cloud cover \nover the western half of Lake Superior and the western third of the \nUP. A few radar echos are noted over western Superior into the MN \nArrowhead. Farther east where skies remain clearer, patchy fog is \ndeveloping across parts of the central and eastern UP, becoming \nlocally dense in a few spots. A few rain showers will clip the far \nwestern UP later this morning, but guidance continues to keep any \nmeasurable precipitation minimal and confined to Gogebic and \nOntonagon Counties, with a around a 25% chance for as much of a \ntenth of an inch around Ironwood today. Several CAMs seem rather \nbullish on these showers making farther east into the central UP \nthis afternoon, but there will be quite a bit of mid level dry air \nto contend with and, as such, have opted to keep the forecast \ncompletely dry for all but the far western UP today. Thus expect \nanother day of highs in the upper 60s to low 70s under partly cloudy \nskies. Minimum RH values could dip into the low 40s or even as low \nas the mid 30s across parts of the central and eastern UP this \nafternoon, but light winds will keep fire weather concerns limited \nas they have been in recent days.\n\nExpect more fog development tonight as skies partially clear out \nthis evening, aided in the west by any precipitation that falls \ntoday. Monday will then be another seasonably warm and dry day as \nhigh pressure lingers over the Great Lakes, with highs in the low to \nperhaps mid 70s in a few spots near the WI border. High pressure \nbreaks down on Tuesday and paves the way for a more progressive \nzonal flow pattern with embedded shortwaves traversing the \nregion through the rest of the week. The first of these will \nimpact the UP late Tuesday into Wednesday as low pressure ejects\nout of the Plains while a shortwave tracks across northern \nOntario, bringing a period of more widespread rain to the UP. \nLatest NBM maintains around a 40% chance for a wetting rain of a\nquarter inch across most of the area through Wednesday morning,\ncloser to 60% across the southcentral UP. Another shortwave \ncrossing northern Ontario on Thursday will bring potential for \nbreezy conditions as well as additional rain chances. \nTemperatures generally look to remain slightly above average in \nthe upper 60s to low 70s through the first half of the week \nbefore eventually settling back into the low to mid 60s later in\nthe week.",
